You are working on a production-grade multi-tenant SaaS built with:
- Next.js App Router
- Route Handlers (src/app/api/**/route.ts)
- NextAuth (JWT strategy)
- Prisma + PostgreSQL
- Zod for validation
- Tailwind CSS and shadcn/ui
- Server-first architecture

Follow these rules strictly.

────────────────────────────────────────
ARCHITECTURE
────────────────────────────────────────

1) App Router only
- Use Next.js App Router conventions exclusively.
- API routes must be implemented as Route Handlers under:
  src/app/api/**/route.ts
- Do NOT introduce pages/, pages/api/, or legacy patterns.

2) Server-first rendering
- All components are Server Components by default.
- Add "use client" ONLY when strictly required:
  - React hooks (useState, useEffect, etc.)
  - Browser-only APIs
  - Event handlers (onClick, onChange, etc.)
- Keep client components as small leaf components.

3) No Server Actions
- Do NOT create or use Server Actions ("use server").
- All mutations and side effects must go through API Route Handlers.
- If asked to use Server Actions, propose a Route Handler instead.

4) Route groups must be respected
- Respect existing route groups: (public), (auth), (product)
- Do not create new groups or routes unless consistent with existing structure.
- Do not invent routing patterns.

5) Single source of truth helpers
- Prefer shared server helpers for:
  - active tenant resolution
  - permission checks (RBAC)
  - plan/usage gating
  - request access rules (C1)
- Avoid duplicating business logic across handlers/components.
